[38;5;231m⏺[39m Design (bounded, ~6 lines of change, all in [38;5;153mapp.js[39m):

  1. [1m[38;5;153mlogin[39m produces the id.[0m It returns [38;5;153m{ success: true, user: username, userId }[39m instead of [38;5;153m{ success: true, user: [39m
     [38;5;153musername }[39m. Since the function is a stub ([38;5;153mapp.js:6[39m — "would POST to [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m in a real app"), the stub
     synthesizes a placeholder id with [38;5;153mcrypto.randomUUID()[39m and carries a comment saying the real implementation reads it
     from the API response. That keeps the shape honest: callers code against a userId that arrives from
     authentication, and swapping the stub for a real fetch later changes only where the value comes from, not the
     contract.
  2. [1mThe caller logs it.[0m [38;5;153mapp.js:23-24[39m already does [38;5;153mconsole.log("Login result:", result)[39m, which picks up the new field
     for free; I'd add one explicit [38;5;153mconsole.log("Logged in user:", result.userId)[39m so the tracking line is deliberate
     rather than incidental.

  No signature change to [38;5;153mlogin[39m, so the single call site keeps working; no change to [38;5;153mindex.html[39m or [38;5;153mvalidateForm[39m.

